A||||24||Cliff Pennock||Stupid salespeople...||||1||
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||08-01-2001||12:06 PM||||Here I am, trying to find a good Home Theater set up for my sister. I ended up buying her an Onkyo DD/DTS Receiver with the Infinity HTS20 speakerset at Circuit City (I know, but for some reason they were even cheaper than online stores). Everything is hooked up and works perfectly now. But I am now convinced that most salespeople at Circuit City/Best Buy/Radio Shack have absolutely no clue what it is they are selling...<P>A few examples. Anybody who has bought any audio/video equipment lately, has undoubtedly been told that you absolutely <B>need</B> Monster Cables. The 24K gold-plated connectors and the special wires should make a world of difference. Well, I've been building Audio/Video setups all my life and I know that this is absolute crock. Perhaps if you hook up a frequency analyzer to your audio system, you <B>might</B> see a slight difference but for simple home setups, it is <B>not</B> audible. Yet they will try anything to sell you these cables which are like 10 times more expensive than any other cable.<P>Radio Shack: I was trying to get a 40' S-Video cable, but the longest they had was 30'. I told the salesperson I needed a 40' cable and he said the longest you could go with an S-Video cable was 30'. Anything above that and the picture would be sh!t. Hmmm, I guess I must be doing something special then because I regularly make 100'-200' S-Video cables for use on shows without any loss in quality. Of course when I told him that, he didn't believe me.<P>Best Buy: I also needed a 40' optical Toslink cable. Radio Shack had 12' cables which could be connected to each other using a special $5 adapter, but the cables were $50 dollars a piece. Best Buy had the same cables for $19 a piece but they didn't have the connectors. I asked them about the connectors to connect two optical Toslink cables and he said they didn't exist. I explained to him nicely they <B>do</B> exist and he then told me that it was not a good thing to do because it would degrade sound-quality. I kindly asked him if he could explain to me how sound degradation would work on an optical-digital cable because in my opinion, since it's digital, it either works or it doesn't. He said it would probably work but the sound would be bad because of the distance (yes, light has a tendancy to get <B>really</B> dim over only 40'  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> not to mention that a bit will degrade in a half-bit or something...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> ).<P>I also liked Circuit City's explanation why I should use a Monster Cable to connect the subwoofer: I needed a uni-directional cable to prevent feedback.<P>I don't know. I guess I must have missed <B>a lot</B> during electronics classes, or perhaps the laws of physics have changed during the past few years...||||reg||1
Z||000001||84Bill||08-01-2001||12:17 PM||||I never did understand this gold plated crap myself. You know it's bad when you can't even find the standard cables anymore. It just seems like another "hype" tactic to get more money out of you.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> And yes they are cueless, and so are the RadioShack people. heheh, just ask them why the connectors are gold and after they give you the preprogrammed answer, ask them if the if the internal wire is gold strand or copper and if they used gold solder on the joints.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000002||Raydar||08-01-2001||12:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>...I also liked Circuit City's explanation why I should use a Monster Cable to connect the subwoofer: I needed a uni-directional cable to prevent feedback.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>ROFL!!!!<P>God, I can't believe the arrogance of these guys. <BR>Do you think they really believe this crap? Or are they just trying for a better commission? <P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||JSocha||08-01-2001||12:27 PM||||Well, in most cases I think they feel you are dumber "then they look".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Loki||08-01-2001||12:37 PM||||I can explain the Monster Cable thing for ya.. There told to.  Its like McDonalds and the "Would like that Super Sized"  They are supposed to try and sell it and at any means possible. Alot of stores around here are in dealings with MC. Awsome stuff dont get me wrong but I understand where your coming from.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||08-01-2001||02:45 PM||||I know most of those places I know that I know more than the guy selling. I get a lot of that 'no one makes that' and show it to them rite on their own shelf. I just had my cable company just come out to check my lines the upper channels wer e coming in very grainy if at all. they replaced the drop clear from down the street but it still sucked. they looked at my system and told me that all the radio shack 'gold' cables and splitters were the problem. They were now digital and they wouldnt work with it. I got a roll of thier cable and a handful of their 'special' spliters. To my surprize it fixed it. What exactly could be the differnces between there cable/splitters and Radio shack ones? isnt it all just wire, shielding no matter where its from? Im amazed but my pic is now fine on all channels.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||08-01-2001||02:57 PM||||I'm guessing it was some sort of an impedence mismatch. <BR>That's geek-speak for a de-tuned condition in the system. As the frequency increased, it became harder for the signal to get through. <BR>When CB radio was really popular, Radio Shack was notorious for having some of the crappiest coaxial cable on the market. Wouldn't surprise me if that were still the case.<BR>Also, all signal splitters are not created equal. Some actually have matching networks inside them. Others reduce the internal parts to the bare minimum. <BR>In many cases, the splitters that you get from the cable company will be superior to the ones you can buy, elsewhere.<BR>They know what it takes to make their system work correctly. Even if they charge "through the nose" for the pieces.<P>Having said all that, this never stopped me from running out and buying my own splitters and cable <BR>(triple shielded RG-6, I think) and wiring my house for cable.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||JSocha||08-01-2001||03:02 PM||||My wife gets a little upset that I do this, but in the end we both get a very good laugh.<P>We'll both go into Best Buy, Sears, Radio Shack, Staples, Office Max, etc., into the computer area.  Me in an old pair of jeans, stained T-shirt, haven't shaved in a week looking poor a@@ to the world.<P>Then I'll play with the computers, pretending I don't know anything about them.  The sales person will come over, ask me if I need help and I go into the spiel that I'm thinking on buying a computer because everyone else is buying one and that I don't know the first thing about them.  At this point, I am their absolute SUCKER without a clue.<P>I'll have them explain things to me like whats a hard drive, processor power (is that good or not), you get the drift.<P>Everytime they think they have a sucker (or they themselves do not know what they are talking about), they will try to sell you the most underpowered, outdated piece of cr@p at a premium price and throw in things that one will never need. Joystick, Encarta maps software (again you get the picture).<P>Then my persona of the poor, computer illiterate person changes to my relm of expertise and I just floor them.  At SEARS this past Christmas (buying a computer for the kids gift story), one salesman, an older guy (early to late 50's), I literally baffled him with my knowledge after he though he had me as a SUCKER in his midst, that I made him turn redder then a tananbaum ornament where he had to go get someone younger that actually knew something (and I do mean something but not a whole lot) about computers just to keep up with me.  Then that kid tried to tell me that I wouldn't find anything for less.  Big mistake on his part as that is what I do for our company, source/spec out computers for our system.<P>Anyways, long story...long, it is just amazing how dumb certain people think others are and/or will take advantage of the indiviual to make a sale.<P>Try it sometime.  It can be absolutely amusing.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||DecadenceR||08-01-2001||04:01 PM||||In best buy, how would he know that the connectors would make it sound like ****  if 2 minutes before that, he told you it didn't exist? If inhis world it doesn't exist, than he should have no real way to know if it would or would not work well....<P>Problem si they employ people in the 16-21 age range that have no clue about anything, and try to BS their way through it. I was stuck in that situation a few times. But I was the one person that would always findout the real answer and learn it instead of make up something on the spot. <BR>Well, usually anyway... LOL||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||08-01-2001||04:06 PM||||A couple of years ago I was in CompUSA, pricing PC systems. Was looking at the ones they custom-build on site. (This was before I started building my own.) <BR>I asked the salesman who the manufacturer was that supplied the motherboards they used in their systems. He couldn't (or wouldn't) tell me. I think those guys are only slightly smarter than the average customer, and way more ignorant than some. Either that or he knew it was a POS brand, and didn't want to say so.<P>I decided to take my checkbook elsewhere. Screw 'em.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||08-01-2001||06:00 PM||||All of this is true, but you're leaving out a very important issue.  Monster Cables look good!  Not that any of my RC cables (is that what they're called?) show, but that's not the point.  If I ever have to pull the cover off of my half built amp rack, I only want the best looking wiring to show.  It's well worth the extra 500% (just in case...).<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000011||AusFiero||08-01-2001||07:17 PM||||Well I feel really dumb now. Anyone got some cables out of the insides of these monsters and some duct tape for me to hold it all together  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>I can relate to the saleman crap though. Like the one that sold me my digital video/stills camera that never did what he said it would. Turned out, quote from salesman when they exchanged it all "I am just a salesman and have been for 40 years, I don't know anything at all about all this new fangled stuff". GREAT! So why did they have him in the department selling electronics when they could have quite as easily moved him to the department selling lawn mowers or selling fridges, or anything but digital technology.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000012||stimpy||08-02-2001||08:33 AM||||Sad thing is, that to really get someone qualified to speak in an informed manner on that technology, that person should have had plenty of exposure to the newest latest and greatest equipment. If a store is paying $7.50 an hour, which one of these nametag wearin guys can afford a high end system?  If stores only hired those technologically savvy enough to know everything, then it would be a very lonely store...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||thomas_l||08-02-2001||09:19 AM||||I had to buy a better s-video cable for a 10 foot run. My DVD player came with one and the picture on my 53" big screen was pretty bad. In fast moving images, the signal wasn't rebuilding the picture fast enough and it looked really blocky. I bought the "medium" quality cable and the problem was solved. You can get well sheilded, low impedence cables without getting the Monsters. A bigger picture and longer cable run will make cheap cables stand out. I didn't have any problem with audio. The fiber from my DVD was only a 3' patch. The wiring out to the speakers is plain old 14 gauge speaker wire. I used 14 because I had some long runs to the rear speakers.<P>I agree with everyone else on one thing tho- some sales people make the buying task total misery. Aside from the ones that baffle you with BS, my hot button is the ones that just glare at you like they are doing you a favor by working there. A real bright spot on my recent trip to Edmonton was a trip to The Mall to pick up a tripod for my camcorder. I went into London Drug which had an awesome camera department. The salesman approached me (wow! I didn't have to find him!) and after a 5 minute explanation of every possible thing to look for in a tripod, actually recommended one of the less expensive models. I was in and out of that store with everything I needed in under 10 minutes. Same thing happened in the Sony store when I needed a spare battery for my camera. And again at my hotel in Calgary. You damn Canadians have ruined me as a customer now that I'm back home!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000014||Shiner||08-02-2001||06:55 PM||||If you think prices for monster cable are outrageous, check out some of the HIGH-high end stuff.  It's absolutely incredible!  Once you get to a certain plateau in the A/V world, it takes GOBS of money to get small increases in performance.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cliff Pennock||08-02-2001||08:59 PM||||It gets better: One of the optical cables I bought at Circuit City turned out to be bad. These cables come with two small adapters to convert the Toslink plug to some kind of other standard. Anyways, I only took the cable (and the receipt) back and asked if they could swap it for a working one. The sales-drone immediately noticed the two adapters were missing and he said he couldn't exchange it because they were missing and he had to return the cable complete to wherever it was they got them from. Well, I asked him if he couldn't simply take a working cable out of another set and swap that for the one I brought. That way, he would have a complete set to return. "No can do" was his answer. So now I have to go back tomorrow with those two little adapters or otherwise I won't get a new cable.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>I dunno... I guess at birth, they accidently threw away the baby and nursed the after-birth.||||reg||1
Z||000016||DJRice||08-02-2001||09:09 PM||||Since your not stereotyping <B><I>all</B></I> salespeople I will step up to the plate and admit that I worked for Radioshack for 4 years while in college earning my BSEE.  <P>I will say, however, that there were precious few who where either college grads or students working for the shack at the time.  And I did have a subscription to "This week in Consumer Electronics" that did a pretty good job of keeping me up to date on the latest gadgets.  <P>As for the monster cables...dont count out the fact that some people are so proud of their A/V equipment that they cant have a regular roll of speaker wire.  They have to have the premium ulta speaker wire with 24k plated plugs.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Cliff Pennock||08-02-2001||10:17 PM||||I don't expect them to be college grads or anything. But in today's world - with the internet 'n all - there's <B>no</B> excuse for being uneducated on the stuff you sell...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Jimmy||08-03-2001||02:37 AM||||I used to work behind the Tech Bench at a Best Buy.  The Managers would seriously get pissed off if I did not swear alleigence to gold cables.  My favorite was the optical and printer cables.  They would tell people there printing quality could degrade if they didn't buy the gold plated cables.  What a crock of SH!T!  I would always ask the fellow employees to explain to me how Ones and Zeros could degrade over a plain cable, or how the Ones and Zeros could be better over a gold cable.  All I ever got was blank stares, figures.<P>Oh and to let everyone know, the reason they push these cables is because if you don't buy them they don't make any money.  Best Buy in particular is in the business of selling cables or "accessories" a gold printer cable would cost you $40 that same cable costs Best Buy a little over $3 dollars.  Thats a $37 dollar profit.  The $300 printer you bought with that cable is ussualy only marked up about $10-$15.  Really, they don't care so much if you buy a printer, all they really want is to have you buy the ink, paper, and GOLD cable.<P>OH and BTW, right before I left the company they started to carry "E-Fast" network cable.  IT has gold connectors so that you can get speeds over 100 on a T100 based lan.  Don't even get me started on that one.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||84Bill||08-03-2001||09:42 AM||||LOL Cliff<BR>I had the same problem with a motherboard return. They wanted me to bring everything back right down to the tab in the AGP slot. I was told that it will take several hours to "TEST" the MB to determine if it was indeed bad. <BR>The lead tech came out and asked me what was wrong and I explained that the bios detects the hard drive but wont boot, he asked me if I repartitioned the drive I said no. Then I asked why I needed to do that his responce was well you had a DMA 33 board before this and the new board is UDMA 100 and the drive needs to be reformatted. I laughed so loud everyone in the store got real quiet for a moment and I again asked to get a new board. The manager said that he could not take it back because someone had returned a damaged board before claiming it was bad and the MFG would not take it back so he had to "eat" the cost. I looked at him squarely and explaind that I got this board in an open box, thats my fault. The board was bad before I go it and now your going to make me eat it? I think not. <BR>By now the store was brimming with people and I begain to raise my voice a few octaves so I could be heard. The manager turned around and got me a replacement board and said there ya go.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I'll never go back to that store again.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Ken Wittlief||08-03-2001||10:56 AM||||oh comeon guys - give the salesman a break<P>thats how they make their money - convincing you that you need to spend more<P>its not a salesman job to sell you what you want - its his job to get as much money out of you as possible - otherwise all the stuff would be on a shelf and there would be no salesmen - it would be like a supermarket.<P>if you dont want to play the game, dont talk to them.<P>I know a guy that worked in a stero shop in the days of vinyl records<P>they had pickups for the turntables that sold for $25, $100, and $1000.<P> the store paid $9, $18 and $27 for them, the rest was all markup.<P> but the reason people bought the $1000 ones, wasnt cause it sounded $900 better than the $100 one - it was because they wanted the best there was. If they had sold the $1000 pickup for $50 then the 'real audiophiles' would go somewhere else and buy the same ones for $1000 - cause you 'cant get a good pickup for less that $1000'.<P>  you gotta know what you want when you walk into an electronics store - and you gotta know what its worth.<P>  personally I buy all my stere equipment at garage sales now. I have a Harmon Kardon receiver that I bought new for $250, and another one a few years older (works and sounds exactly the same) that I bought at a garage sale for $5.||||reg||11
Z||000021||Raydar||08-03-2001||12:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>...if you dont want to play the game, dont talk to them.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sometimes that's easier said than done. <BR>Walk into Circuit City or Best Buy near my house, and they circle like sharks. <BR>Until you actually *want* something, then they scurry away like roaches, and hide under the furniture.  <P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000022||84Bill||08-03-2001||01:09 PM||||LOL Raydar!<BR>do what I do, carry the "Sonic BUG-A-Way" by RONCO, switch it on only when you don't get what you want to hear  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>Or just leave it on and watch them bump into walls and stuff.||||reg||14
Z||000023||Ken Wittlief||08-03-2001||02:54 PM||||or better yet, detune it down to about 19,000Hz, so it makes their ears ring whenever they are near you, and hide it under your jacket.<P>  19,000Hz at about 120dB will give almost anyone a headache in about 5 minutes - so make sure you wear earplugs.||||reg||11
Z||000024||DJRice||08-03-2001||03:41 PM||||I used to sell people the cheaper stuff, much to the chagrin of some management members.  But I was selling a heckuva lot more of that then anyone else was selling of the premo-gold cabling and If I sold 5 sets of plain A/V patch cords at $3.99 each in the time it takes another salesman to <B><I>try</B></I> to convice someone that the $19.99 cable is the one they needed, I figured I was ahead of the game.  Turns out I was consistenly ranked in the Top 5 sales people for the North Florida (Jacksonville area) district for most of the time I worked there....in a small strip mall store.<P>If you think buying cables is bad, try cell phone shopping......<P>--Dillon||||reg||1
